  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather prevails this week with continued \nseasonable temperatures. The holiday weekend favors dry weather, \nthough watching late Sunday into Monday for a chance of rain.\n\nCool, dry, NW flow persists today thanks to surface high pressure \nmoving over NY by the afternoon and over SNE tonight into Wednesday. \nThis will slacken the pressure gradient leading to a steady decrease \nin the breeziness we've seen of late. High pressure supports sunny \nskies and cold advection brings a cooler airmass overhead meaning \nhighs won't get out of the 50s for most today as 925 mb temps\ndrop from 11-13C on Monday to 5-7C on Tuesday. The high's \ninfluence will be felt most overnight as clear skies and calm \nwinds lead to efficient radiational cooling and much cooler low \ntemperatures. Leaning on MOS guidance which handles these \nregimes better than other guidance, we should see fairly \nwidespread lows in the 33-37F range. As such, a Frost Advisory \nis in effect for the typically coolest spots. \n\nIt will be a brief cool down as a vertically stacked low near the \nHudson Bay will send a weak trough through the northeast Wed into \nThursday backing flow to the SW and pumping up temperatures into the \nmid 60s by Wednesday and low to mid 70s by Thursday. With this comes \nsome moisture advection as well leading to higher humidity on \nThursday ahead of the cold front. However, lacking good dynamics and \nmoisture, current thinking is that Thursday evening's cold front is \nmostly dry. High pressure then builds back into New England for the \nFriday/Saturday period bringing sunny skies and seasonable \ntemperatures in the 60s. This ridge of high pressure should preclude \na disturbance passing to our north Sunday from affecting SNE. \nHowever, we'll continue to monitor the remnants of what is \ncurrently AL92 in the Gulf which may dampen the end of our \nholiday weekend; confidence in this outcome is quite low, \nthough, as high pressure could very well deflect it to the \nsouth.",
